WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:03.889
Thank you.

00:00:03.889 --> 00:00:12.850
It's a great pleasure to see all of you here
tonight.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:18.650
The festival has had conversations about science
and religion over the years.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:20.710
Perhaps some of you have come to some of those.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:28.050
And oftentimes, there are two sides represented
in that conversation and sometimes the two

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:34.570
sides, you know, science and religion, sometimes
they're contentious, sometimes they're harmonious,

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:36.710
but tonight we're doing something differently.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:40.050
We really only have one side here tonight.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:44.690
So the group of people who are going to come
out for this discussion, they're all scientists.

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:49.430
They all come from the background of science,
but our goal is to see if by walking this

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:53.970
one side, this one trajectory of science,
we can gain some illumination into the other

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.350
side.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:01:00.010
Into the side of religion side of faith.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:07.560
Before I bring out our esteemed group of panelists,
I just want to set some context and to do

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:12.439
so, I'm going to begin with something which
is presumably familiar to many of you.

00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:21.929
So this is what a beautiful midnight sky brimming
with stars looks like in New York City.

00:01:21.929 --> 00:01:28.920
Now, I also have a little cabin, Upstate New
York in the Catskill Mountains and when I'm

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:34.849
up there and it's a nice, dark night sky,
I can look up and see something that looks

00:01:34.849 --> 00:01:38.390
just like this.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:39.390
Maybe not just like this.

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:42.130
This is takes a Hubble Space Telescope, you
know?

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:46.529
But you get the idea and when you see a wondrous
sky like this, you can't help but ask yourself,

00:01:46.529 --> 00:01:48.189
how does it all work?

00:01:48.189 --> 00:01:51.789
How did it all come to be?

00:01:51.789 --> 00:01:57.000
And I have spent part of my professional life
trying to advance the scientific understanding

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.090
of some of these questions, and because I
work on the more mathematical end of physics,

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:06.109
when I look up, I tend to see order and harmony
in a peculiar language.

00:02:06.109 --> 00:02:10.130
The language of mathematics, a language of
symbols.

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:16.310
But, many others, when they look up at a sky
like this, it brings to mind other things,

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:17.310
right?

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:20.480
Ideas of soul, of eternity, of divinity, of
God.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.930
And for some, that kind of talk, it feels
kind of loose or vague.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:27.700
For some, it's even off-putting.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:35.480
But when you look at the data, you see something
utterly remarkable, right here in the 21st

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:41.260
century, the modern technological age and
we have long since cracked the atom, explored

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:46.480
the surface of Mars, detected gravitational
waves and so much more.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.720
There are still many of us who are believers.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:56.230
So if we look at some of the numbers, about
say 2.2 billion of us identify as Christians.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.470
About 1.7 billion is Muslims.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:04.450
Hindus, Buddhists, that gives us another two
billion, plus, if we throw in my little tribe,

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.240
it's about 14 million, right?

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:12.850
And then if we add in the atheists, this takes
us to one and half billion which is just to

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:19.310
say there's a lot of people on this planet
who would look to the heavens and think of

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:20.650
heaven.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:26.010
So if aliens were able to sweep down toward
planet Earth, and let's say they had some

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:31.740
wondrous equipment that allowed them to detect
religious belief, to give us a kind of heat

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:35.410
map of faith, this is what our planet would
look like.

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:38.070
You could probably work out the color scheme
for yourself.

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:41.150
Blue is Protestant, Red is Catholic and so
forth.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:42.530
You get the idea.

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:45.670
We are a religious planet.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:52.210
Personally, I am not religious in any conventional
sense, but I do consider myself spiritual

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:55.440
and I certainly do consider myself curious.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:00.100
One thing that I have certainly gotten ever
more curious about is why do we believe?

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:08.650
Now, the simplest answer is we have religious
belief because what religion tells us is true.

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:12.459
That raises a whole lot of challenges that
we're all familiar with and perhaps the most

00:04:12.459 --> 00:04:18.729
relevant for tonight's discussion is there
are over 4000 distinct religions practiced

00:04:18.729 --> 00:04:23.750
on Earth and if we just take one of them,
say we parse Christianity a little more finely,

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:28.069
there are over 33,000 distinct denominations.

00:04:28.069 --> 00:04:31.340
They can't all be right.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:35.969
So the natural supposition is that at most
one of them is right, which would mean that

00:04:35.969 --> 00:04:42.669
if Sarah here, happy in her own beliefs, she
denies therefor the beliefs of all others,

00:04:42.669 --> 00:04:48.330
like Terrik over here who again, happy in
his own faith, denies the validity of all

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:53.189
others and that goes true for Pim and for
Ofryim and also for Amalyia and it

00:04:53.189 --> 00:04:59.849
even holds for, say this guy over here, Richard,
who not only denies in the validity of all

00:04:59.849 --> 00:05:03.069
other beliefs, he denies the validity all
beliefs.

00:05:03.069 --> 00:05:10.050
We may be a believing planet, but most of
us deny the validity of most beliefs, which

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:16.830
means that even if Sarah holds to her religion
because it is true, she still needs to explain

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:21.930
why everybody else holds to their own misguided
faiths.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:23.770
And that holds true for everybody else.

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:27.479
So this takes us to a simple but remarkable
conclusion.

00:05:27.479 --> 00:05:34.749
Normally, the discussion of science and religions,
you know, it all comes down to what's right,

00:05:34.749 --> 00:05:37.629
what's wrong, what's true, what's false.

00:05:37.629 --> 00:05:42.979
But here we see that even if a given religion
is true, it hardly changes the question at

00:05:42.979 --> 00:05:44.219
all.

00:05:44.219 --> 00:05:51.100
We still need to ask why it is that so many
of us have a tendency to believe.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:54.930
We have to ask yourself, what is it about
the human species that drives us to find order

00:05:54.930 --> 00:06:02.499
and meaning and, in particular, to find the
turn toward the supernatural so utterly natural.

00:06:02.499 --> 00:06:10.249
1936, this guy over here, Albert Einstein
wrote a letter to a school girl named Phyllis

00:06:10.249 --> 00:06:16.599
who had asked Einstein about his own religious
beliefs.

00:06:16.599 --> 00:06:20.960
Everyone who is seriously involved in the
pursuit of science becomes convinced that

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.349
some spirit is manifested in the laws of the
universe.

00:06:24.349 --> 00:06:27.960
One that is vastly superior to that of man.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:33.999
In this way the pursuit of science leads to
a religious feeling of a special sort which

00:06:33.999 --> 00:06:39.780
is surely quite different from the religiosity
of someone more naïve.

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:45.569
Much has been made about Einstein's use of
this phrase, religious feeling, but his later

00:06:45.569 --> 00:06:52.960
writings made very clear that he was speaking
of an abstract spirituality, not a conventional

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:53.960
religion.

00:06:53.960 --> 00:07:00.120
The word of God is, for me, nothing more than
the expression and product of human weaknesses.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:06.430
The bible a collection of honorable, but still
primitive legends which are nevertheless pretty

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:07.690
childish.

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:12.460
No interpretation no matter how subtle can,
for me, change this.

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:18.460
Charles Darwin, the Father of Evolution by
natural selection, he allowed for the possibility

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:20.540
of God.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:23.610
I have never denied the existence of God.

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:28.090
I think the theory of evolution is fully compatible
with faith in God.

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:33.659
I think the greatest argument for the existence
in God is the impossibility of demonstrating

00:07:33.659 --> 00:07:40.469
and understanding that the immense universe,
sublime above all measure and man, were the

00:07:40.469 --> 00:07:42.910
result of chance.

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:48.360
At the same time, Darwin also noted that a
religious belief, a religious sensibility

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:53.990
could emerge from the interplay between biological
and cultural evolution.

00:07:53.990 --> 00:08:00.580
Nor must we overlook the probability of the
constant inculcation in a belief of God on

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:07.119
the minds of children, producing so strong
and perhaps an inherited effect on their brains,

00:08:07.119 --> 00:08:13.210
not yet fully developed that it would be as
difficult for them to throw off their belief

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:18.761
in God as for a monkey to throw off its instinctive
fear and hatred of a snake.

00:08:18.761 --> 00:08:26.409
The Dalai Lama has his own iconic perspective
on these issues.

00:08:26.409 --> 00:08:32.560
Both Buddhism and modern science shared a
deep suspicion of any notion of absolutes,

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:38.680
whether conceptualized as a transcendent being,
as an eternal unchanging principal such as

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:43.060
soul, or as a fundamental substratum of reality.

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:48.700
Both Buddhism and science prefer to account
for the evolution and emergence of the cosmos

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:54.850
and life in terms of the complex interrelations
of the natural laws of cause and effect.

00:08:54.850 --> 00:09:01.200
From the methodological perspective, both
the traditions emphasize the role of empiricism.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:06.900
In the Buddhist investigation of reality,
at least in principle, empirical evidence

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:13.000
should triumph over scriptural authority,
no matter how deeply venerate a scripture

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:15.150
may be.

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:20.160
Years ago, I had the pleasure of sharing the
stage with the Dalai Lama in an event that

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:25.990
took place down in Texas and I had an opportunity
to ask him a question.

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:31.630
The question is asked was, I said, "Look,
there are all these books out there that make

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:38.750
the case that what we're doing in modern physics
is somehow a recapitulation or a reflection

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:43.520
of ideas that ultimately find their origin
in eastern religious thought."

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:45.540
So I asked him, "Is this true?

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:46.700
Is this your perspective?"

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:53.770
And he very forthrightly said, he said, "Look,
when it comes to questions of consciousness,

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:57.520
that's where we have something to offer science."

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:00.790
But he said, "When it comes to understanding
the fundamental laws and the particles and

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:08.600
all that detail about how the world actually
works," he said, "We need to look to science."

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:14.980
So it was a kind of remarkable moment where
this great spiritual leader showed this remarkable

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:19.060
and broad embrace of science.

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:27.350
At the same time there are great scientists
who show a similar embrace of religious thought.

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:31.030
Here's Nobel Laureate, William Phillips.

00:10:31.030 --> 00:10:34.960
The point is that there are plenty of scientists
who see no difficulty in being serious about

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:38.470
their science and serious about their faith.

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:43.300
I know plenty of others, and you’ve see
the statistics that support that idea, but

00:10:43.300 --> 00:10:49.810
nevertheless there is a common misperception
in society that this isn’t the case.

00:10:49.810 --> 00:10:53.290
And here's Francis Collins, head of the National
Institutes of Health.

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:59.150
I think most people are actually kind of comfortable
with the idea that science is a reliable way

00:10:59.150 --> 00:11:04.780
to learn about nature, but it's not the whole
story and there's a place also for religion,

00:11:04.780 --> 00:11:07.910
for faith, for theology, for philosophy.

00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:11.670
But that harmony perspective doesn't get as
much attention.

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:16.340
Nobody's as interested in harmony as they
are in conflict, I'm afraid.

00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:23.040
2015, Pew Research Foundation found that the
percent of Americans that agreed with the

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:29.060
statement that science and religion are often
in conflict, they found that agreement with

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:35.290
that was almost 60% and that again is often
how the conversation is framed.

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:36.800
Science versus religion.

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:38.000
That is an important question.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:41.800
It may come up here tonight, but it's not
the focus of what we're talking about here

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:42.800
tonight.

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:47.930
And so we're asking ourselves, can we use
science to illuminate religion?

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:54.190
Can we gain some understanding of why people
have a need to look to a power beyond themselves,

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:55.450
beyond the laws of physics?

00:11:55.450 --> 00:11:59.880
Is that need written into our DNA?

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:04.060
The natural selection for that kind of worldview,
right?

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:09.990
Why in the world does this world have so many
brains that want to believe?

00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:11.540
That's the question.

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:17.620
And to deal with this question, try to gain
some insight, we have a great group of thinkers

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:20.880
and I'd like to now bring them out to the
stage.

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:25.890
Our first participant is professor emerita
from the College of William and Mary, where

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:30.480
she taught anthropology for 28 years, author
of numerous books, including Personalities

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:31.480
on the Plate, How Animals Grieve, and Evolving
God.

00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:32.620
Please join me in welcoming our first guest
... Barbara King on the fly.

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:44.290
Our next guest is a research scientist at
NYU Langone Medical Center.

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:49.910
He's also professor of cognitive and affective
neuroscience at NYU, co-founded the Nonduality

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:52.660
Institute where he is the principle science
investigator.

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:56.320
Please join me in welcoming neuroscience,
Zoran Josipovic.

00:12:56.320 --> 00:13:03.560
Also with us tonight is a university distinguished
professor of psychology at Northeastern University

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:07.220
with appointments at Harvard Medical School
and the Mass General Hospital.

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:12.480
In addition to the book, How Emotions Are
Made, she has published over 100 scholarly

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:13.480
papers.

00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:15.210
Please join me in welcoming Lisa Barrett.

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:17.130
All right, finally.

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:23.240
Our guest is the Johnstone Professor of Psychology
at Harvard University, a two-time Pulitzer

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:27.010
prize finalist and author of the bestselling
books including, How the Mind Works and The

00:13:27.010 --> 00:13:31.950
Language Instinct, a pioneer and champion
of evolutionary psychology, named one of Time

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:36.960
Magazine's 100 Most Influential People, please
welcome Steven Pinker.

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:44.290
All right, so we're going to have a pretty
free form discussion here, where we're going

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:51.110
to try to address some of these questions
and we're going to organize the discussion

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:53.730
into three parts, roughly speaking.

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:57.520
A kind of trinity of parts, befitting for
tonight's discussion.

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:02.660
We're going to talk about some of the history
of religious belief.

00:14:02.660 --> 00:14:07.100
We're going to talk about the longevity, the
fact that this is something that has stuck

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:08.850
with us for some time.

00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:15.120
Then we're going to focus on the benefit,
if at all, for this kind of way of interacting

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:16.560
with the world.

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:22.090
What I'd like to do before getting started,
if you don't mind, especially since it's a

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:26.660
nice small group here, it's good to get a
sense of where people are coming from in this

00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:31.910
kind of discussion, so if we could just sort
of go one by one, just sort of give us a sense

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:37.120
of where you ... We'll do it ... If you don't
mind, and you don't have to, but if you're

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:43.840
willing to share it, just a couple of words
on where you come from in the religious spectrum.

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:47.860
Steven, you willing to just say a few words?

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:49.780
You mean our own beliefs personally?

00:14:49.780 --> 00:14:50.780
If you don't mind.

00:14:50.780 --> 00:14:52.060
You don't have to, but if you're willing to.

00:14:52.060 --> 00:14:53.060
Yeah.

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:58.100
Well, I don't believe in the existence of
supernatural entities, including God, souls,

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:01.930
spirits, genies, devils, and so on.

00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:06.360
I am a ... I belong to the same tribe as you.

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:14.980
I'm Jewish and appreciate many of the iconography,
the traditions, the community of my own and

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:18.920
other cultural groups, but that doesn't mean
you have to sign on to the content, and I

00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:19.920
don't.

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:20.920
Right.

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:21.920
Lisa.

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:26.100
I would say Steve pretty summed it up pretty
well for me too.

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:35.990
We practice some rituals in our home as sort
of, I don't know, not exactly archeological

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:41.430
artifacts, but they are kind of artifacts
of the past, you know?

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:47.110
If we decide to light candles on Friday night,
I'm using candlesticks that my great-grandmother

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:53.930
schlepped from Russia and that people have
been doing this for over 5000 years, and that's

00:15:53.930 --> 00:15:55.130
meaningful.

00:15:55.130 --> 00:16:02.170
I also think that Judaism is an interesting
moral code that is somewhat ... emphasizes

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:09.529
somewhat more behavior over intent, which
is appealing to us in some ways.

00:16:09.529 --> 00:16:15.740
I would say we're ... colloquially we're atheists
as a ... definitely in our house, although

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:22.240
we do have trappings of, as I said, of ritual
in the way that I described.

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:23.240
Yep.

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:24.240
Zoran.

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:30.840
I was raised as atheist, but later discovered
that really my family believed in scientism.

00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:33.360
Like, science has an answer to everything.

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:38.240
It's a form of religion, I think for some
people.

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:47.050
Personally, I have practiced meditation for
over 35 years and I'm mostly interested in

00:16:47.050 --> 00:16:52.440
this mystical unitary states are known to
us as the consciousness where people experience

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:58.060
both unitary consciousness, either alone or
unitary consciousness with experience.

00:16:58.060 --> 00:17:01.690
I'm interested what it does to a person and
what it does to the brain.

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:02.690
Right.

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:03.690
Barbara.

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:08.320
Growing up in New Jersey, I was raised as
a Presbyterian, spent a fair amount of time

00:17:08.320 --> 00:17:09.320
in church.

00:17:09.320 --> 00:17:11.730
I know identify also as an atheist.

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:17.819
When I travel, I do find myself drawn to churches,
to sitting in the stillness of a church and

00:17:17.819 --> 00:17:20.480
to looking at the art and the architecture.

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:25.280
I think that is a beautiful part of our history,
but I do that as an atheist.

00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:33.520
And for my own sense of spirituality, I go
to a Springsteen concert.

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:34.520
Right.

00:17:34.520 --> 00:17:44.580
So, you know, there are some curious human
behaviors that strike us as unusual, like,

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:48.660
I think ... I don't know how much of this
is true, but Beethoven is said to have always

00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:51.770
dunked his head in a bucket of ice water every
morning.

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:56.440
Ben Franklin is said to have stood naked in
front of an open window every morning.

00:17:56.440 --> 00:18:02.470
Nikola Tesla, you know, a great champion and
iconic scientific figure, apparently used

00:18:02.470 --> 00:18:06.370
to curl his toes a hundred times each night
before going to sleep.

00:18:06.370 --> 00:18:10.610
So you sort of hear those, you raise your
eyebrows, it's kind of curious and so on.

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:15.530
But, we don't feel the need to explain that
kind of behavior, but when it comes to a behavior

00:18:15.530 --> 00:18:22.580
that is pervasive and that lasts for thousands
of years, then it feels like it deserves an

00:18:22.580 --> 00:18:26.720
explanation and that's really why we're having
this conversation here tonight.

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:28.930
So, maybe start with you, Barbara.

00:18:28.930 --> 00:18:35.130
When I hear the word faith or religion, my
mind automatically goes toward one of the

00:18:35.130 --> 00:18:39.870
major religions that are practiced in the
world today.

00:18:39.870 --> 00:18:41.970
Is that too limited of you?

00:18:41.970 --> 00:18:47.600
Yeah, I think it's a very natural view, but
speaking anthropologically, if we were to

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:54.800
do that heat sensing map of the world, we
would see people who not only believe in God,

00:18:54.800 --> 00:19:00.690
or don't believe in God, but also many, many
people who believe in gods, plural, spirits

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:05.660
in the forest, venerate ancestors, or have
an enormous range of beliefs.

00:19:05.660 --> 00:19:11.670
So, I think broadening our view to understand
that there's numbers of ways, not just in

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:15.800
the past but now, to believe is a very helpful
starting point.

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:21.559
Now, you've also done work where you've gone
beyond this species, right?

00:19:21.559 --> 00:19:23.040
Absolutely, yes.

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:28.670
My work is in animals and there's a fascinating
conversation going on now about whether it

00:19:28.670 --> 00:19:35.270
is reasonable to suggest that other animals
than us, do have a sense of either spirituality

00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:39.830
or religiosity and there's very invigorated
debate going on.

00:19:39.830 --> 00:19:44.421
You know, Jane Goodall was the very first
person to suggest, as far as I'm aware, that

00:19:44.421 --> 00:19:46.090
chimpanzees may be spiritual.

00:19:46.090 --> 00:19:48.559
But this is continued over decades.

00:19:48.559 --> 00:19:49.760
This is not my view.

00:19:49.760 --> 00:19:53.870
I am not suggesting that chimpanzees are spiritual
or religious.

00:19:53.870 --> 00:19:59.430
Where I come in is suggesting that their behavior
is an evolutionary platform, so that what

00:19:59.430 --> 00:20:03.530
we see in our closest living relative gives
us and understanding of the building blocks

00:20:03.530 --> 00:20:06.250
of what later became our religiosity.

00:20:06.250 --> 00:20:12.940
So, we know that chimpanzees, for example
at a waterfall, can show what we might understand

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:15.860
as a sense of awe and wonder.

00:20:15.860 --> 00:20:21.510
We know that chimpanzees can take the perspective
of another through theory of mind.

00:20:21.510 --> 00:20:25.600
We know that they can show empathy, compassion,
that they have their own rituals and their

00:20:25.600 --> 00:20:26.800
own rules.

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:32.550
And so I think that we wouldn't be where we
are today without our primate past, which

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:37.050
includes, of course, not only living apes,
but what we'll talk about later I imagine,

00:20:37.050 --> 00:20:39.010
other human ancestors.

00:20:39.010 --> 00:20:40.730
Early Homo sapiens, Neanderthals.

00:20:40.730 --> 00:20:46.410
So, just as culture evolved, language evolved
and technology evolved, I believe that that

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:48.780
human religious imagination evolved.

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:57.250
So, Steve, part of what we're doing here is
trying to think about behavior and think about

00:20:57.250 --> 00:21:02.440
evolution and sort of how they can play off
of each other, and I know that the field of

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:07.460
evolution in psychology is dedicated to trying
to make those kinds of connections precise.

00:21:07.460 --> 00:21:12.809
Can you just give us a sense of what evolutionary
psychology actually does and how it can give

00:21:12.809 --> 00:21:15.730
insight into these kinds of issues?

00:21:15.730 --> 00:21:23.970
Well, the brain, like other complex organs,
owes its non-random organization to natural

00:21:23.970 --> 00:21:24.970
selection.

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:31.809
That if there are circuits in the brain that
accomplish improbable feats, then natural

00:21:31.809 --> 00:21:35.230
selection is the explanation for how they
got wired up the way they are.

00:21:35.230 --> 00:21:42.930
And we're going to ask of various psychological
features whether they are adaptations.

00:21:42.930 --> 00:21:48.940
That is, whether they increased the chances
of reproduction in our ancestors.

00:21:48.940 --> 00:21:52.950
For a lot of psychological features that's
pretty straightforward to do.

00:21:52.950 --> 00:21:59.160
It's no mystery why we see in stereo, because
it's a ... for many reasons, highly adaptive

00:21:59.160 --> 00:22:01.430
to get a sense of the third dimension.

00:22:01.430 --> 00:22:07.200
Why we're repulsed by kinds of substances
that are likely to carry disease.

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:09.580
Why we find certain partners sexually attractive.

00:22:09.580 --> 00:22:11.590
For religion, it's a religious belief.

00:22:11.590 --> 00:22:15.280
For supernatural belief, it's not so obvious.

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:20.440
I don't think there's any accepted theory
that religious belief, per se, is an adaptation.

00:22:20.440 --> 00:22:25.690
Rather, it can be a by-product of other adaptations.

00:22:25.690 --> 00:22:32.240
In particular, the ability to attribute minds
to other people.

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:35.350
We can't literally get inside people's heads.

00:22:35.350 --> 00:22:43.050
A mind is invisible, colorless, odorless,
tasteless, but we couldn't survive as social

00:22:43.050 --> 00:22:46.950
beings unless we assume that other people
had minds as we do.

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:50.520
We interpret their behavior in terms of their
beliefs and desires.

00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:56.560
From there it may be a short step to attribute
minds to entities that aren't other human

00:22:56.560 --> 00:23:01.930
beings, such as to trees and rivers and the
wind, in which case we call it animism.

00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:07.850
We attribute minds to inanimate entities,
to our own artifacts, in which case we call

00:23:07.850 --> 00:23:08.850
it idolatry.

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:15.260
Or to no hunk of matter in particular, in
which we call it ... in case we call it spiritualism.

00:23:15.260 --> 00:23:23.720
Disembodied souls and spirits and father-like
entities that don't have any material existence,

00:23:23.720 --> 00:23:27.570
but have this thing that we naturally attribute
to one another.

00:23:27.570 --> 00:23:29.090
So it would be an extension.

00:23:29.090 --> 00:23:35.221
One would then have to explain why the adaptation
of attributing minds to others, sometimes

00:23:35.221 --> 00:23:40.770
called theory of mind, or mentalizing or mind
reading, or intuitive psychology, why should

00:23:40.770 --> 00:23:48.160
be so easy to overextend it to entities that
aren't in fact brains.

00:23:48.160 --> 00:23:55.800
And there, part of the answer comes from experience,
what kind of input do we have in living our

00:23:55.800 --> 00:24:01.470
lives that makes this belief congenial and
a number of anthropologists have pointed out

00:24:01.470 --> 00:24:07.770
that before the advent of modern neuroscience,
the idea that minds can exist independently

00:24:07.770 --> 00:24:09.720
of brains was not so farfetched.

00:24:09.720 --> 00:24:13.170
There's actually some compelling and empirical
data.

00:24:13.170 --> 00:24:19.200
Edward Tyler I think was the originator of
this observation, that when we dream for example,

00:24:19.200 --> 00:24:24.290
it's apparent that some part of us is up and
about, walking around in the world and our

00:24:24.290 --> 00:24:26.290
body's in bed the whole time.

00:24:26.290 --> 00:24:32.320
A natural hypothesis is that our ... some
locus of experience is not wedded to the body,

00:24:32.320 --> 00:24:33.610
but can part company from it.

00:24:33.610 --> 00:24:39.020
Or in death, if someone suddenly collapses,
they may look identical to the way they were

00:24:39.020 --> 00:24:42.940
a few minutes ago, but something seems to
have left their body that animated shortly

00:24:42.940 --> 00:24:43.940
beforehand.

00:24:43.940 --> 00:24:50.390
And reflections in still water, shadows, seem
to capture the essence of a person, including

00:24:50.390 --> 00:24:55.010
their activity, their expressions, their goal
directed actions.

00:24:55.010 --> 00:25:00.559
And again, divorced from the actual hunk of
flesh.

00:25:00.559 --> 00:25:07.740
If you're in a trance from lack of sleep,
or a fever, or a drug, again, the experience

00:25:07.740 --> 00:25:11.750
is that your mind can part company from your
body.

00:25:11.750 --> 00:25:16.571
So, if you combine those experiences with
our natural habit of attributing minds, it's

00:25:16.571 --> 00:25:22.050
not farfetched to think that minds can exist
separately from bodies.

00:25:22.050 --> 00:25:23.050
Now we know better.

00:25:23.050 --> 00:25:30.200
We know that the brain is the locus of experience,
that there are many ways in which the brain

00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:35.470
can be vulnerable to illusions, dreaming being
an obvious case.

00:25:35.470 --> 00:25:39.290
There's brain activity when we're asleep and
that's why we experience things.

00:25:39.290 --> 00:25:42.580
But, before modern neuroscience, it wasn't
such a crazy belief.

00:25:42.580 --> 00:25:47.030
One other ingredient is that we depend for
our beliefs on other people, on experts.i

00:25:47.030 --> 00:25:52.190
believe a lot of things that I have no basis
for believing in my own experience.

00:25:52.190 --> 00:25:53.190
Like quantum physics.

00:25:53.190 --> 00:25:54.190
Yeah, like superstrings.

00:25:54.190 --> 00:25:55.700
I really believe-
You believe in superstrings?

00:25:55.700 --> 00:26:00.680
I do, because very smart people tell me that
they exist and I trust them.

00:26:00.680 --> 00:26:03.010
I don't say they exist.

00:26:03.010 --> 00:26:04.010
They may exist.

00:26:04.010 --> 00:26:05.010
That they may exist.

00:26:05.010 --> 00:26:07.360
I give some non-zero probability of that.

00:26:07.360 --> 00:26:13.090
That opens up a niche for people to market
all kinds of beliefs about unobservable entities

00:26:13.090 --> 00:26:19.070
including gods and messiahs, and devils and
so on, and a whole set of questions which

00:26:19.070 --> 00:26:25.020
I won't talk about now is, what are the incentives
for the purveyors of supernatural beliefs?

00:26:25.020 --> 00:26:28.850
What's in it for them to get other people
to believe in gods and souls and spirits.

00:26:28.850 --> 00:26:31.500
There are plenty of reasons, but that's the
other part of the story.

00:26:31.500 --> 00:26:37.980
Now presume the overactive assigning of agency
out to the world is better than an underactive

00:26:37.980 --> 00:26:39.159
version of it, right?

00:26:39.159 --> 00:26:44.560
If you're walking around and there's a rock
and you happen to think that it has a mind,

00:26:44.560 --> 00:26:48.280
so be it, but if you're walking around and
there's a snake and you don't think it has

00:26:48.280 --> 00:26:51.510
a mind, you don't think it can attack you,
that's probably not a good thing.

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:56.700
So, evolutionary speaking, presumably this
overactive assigning of agency has adaptive

00:26:56.700 --> 00:26:57.700
value.

00:26:57.700 --> 00:26:58.700
Possibly.

00:26:58.700 --> 00:27:00.310
It's not so clear.

00:27:00.310 --> 00:27:07.110
If it involves making sacrifices that are
ultimately irrational, if it involves being

00:27:07.110 --> 00:27:11.130
manipulated by others, maybe not.

00:27:11.130 --> 00:27:16.040
But it may just be that the overall benefit
of being able to attribute minds outweighs

00:27:16.040 --> 00:27:19.980
the cost in cases where others can exploit
us.

00:27:19.980 --> 00:27:24.350
In the case of animals, of course, animals
actually do have minds so it's not such a

00:27:24.350 --> 00:27:25.460
crazy thing.

00:27:25.460 --> 00:27:32.169
Indeed, a lot of ... In some hunter-gatherer
peoples, they do attribute enormous amounts

00:27:32.169 --> 00:27:35.799
of intentionality to the animals they hunt
and with good reason, 'cause the animals really

00:27:35.799 --> 00:27:40.720
are trying to escape them for the same reason
that we try to escape from threats.

00:27:40.720 --> 00:27:44.260
So, that degree of extension is not so farfetched.

00:27:44.260 --> 00:27:48.600
It's when it comes to rocks and rivers and
mountains and trees and wind, that it becomes

00:27:48.600 --> 00:27:49.600
more problematic.

00:27:49.600 --> 00:27:50.600
Right.

00:27:50.600 --> 00:27:59.390
So, Lisa, what is your view in terms of are
we at some level wired for belief, or is that

00:27:59.390 --> 00:28:01.430
not an important part of the equation?

00:28:01.430 --> 00:28:04.500
I think it is actually.

00:28:04.500 --> 00:28:10.810
When we say ... When you ask are we wired
for beliefs, I think that that can mean a

00:28:10.810 --> 00:28:12.810
couple of different things, right?

00:28:12.810 --> 00:28:20.461
So, in a sense, you could say, well, all brains,
actually every brain on this planet, to some

00:28:20.461 --> 00:28:29.080
extent, is wired to make predictions about
what's going to happen next based on what's

00:28:29.080 --> 00:28:30.670
happened in the past.

00:28:30.670 --> 00:28:38.530
So, brains are not wired to react to things
in the world, they're wired to predict.

00:28:38.530 --> 00:28:41.980
It's metabolically efficient to predict.

00:28:41.980 --> 00:28:48.429
Physiologically, most of the biological systems
we have in the body are predictive to some

00:28:48.429 --> 00:28:49.580
extent.

00:28:49.580 --> 00:28:56.680
And so, if you mean ... A lot of people talk
about predictions where ... When I say prediction

00:28:56.680 --> 00:29:04.280
I mean, our brains for example, change the
firing of their own neurons in advance of

00:29:04.280 --> 00:29:06.600
sensory input arriving to the brain.

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:09.750
That's how you're understanding the words
that I'm speaking to you right now.

00:29:09.750 --> 00:29:15.880
You've had a lifetime of experience of patterns,
encoding patterns of what these sounds refer

00:29:15.880 --> 00:29:20.770
to and the patterns in their temporal contingencies.

00:29:20.770 --> 00:29:26.250
All brains work like this and if you believe
that a prediction is like a belief, which

00:29:26.250 --> 00:29:32.570
scientists do write about predictions this
way, as if they are beliefs or explanations

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:39.170
that are preemptively offered to anticipate
and explain incoming sensory inputs, then

00:29:39.170 --> 00:29:41.130
yes, we are wired.

00:29:41.130 --> 00:29:44.750
Another way in which we're wired, you could
say, is that-

00:29:44.750 --> 00:29:48.770
But that's for belief in things presumably
that are demonstrably true.

00:29:48.770 --> 00:29:52.720
That's belief in any case, right?

00:29:52.720 --> 00:30:01.720
So, the idea that the brain is wired for prediction
as opposed to reaction, is a general explanation,

00:30:01.720 --> 00:30:08.060
it's a general computational approach to understanding
meaning making of any sort.

00:30:08.060 --> 00:30:17.350
So, that means making meaning of fluctuating
changes in light, which you experience as

00:30:17.350 --> 00:30:19.480
sights, as vision.

00:30:19.480 --> 00:30:26.710
It's making meaning of fluctuating changes
in air pressure, which you experience as sounds.

00:30:26.710 --> 00:30:38.580
And it's also making meaning of changes that
happen longer ... longer temporal sensory

00:30:38.580 --> 00:30:45.660
changes which we would think of as an episode
or an event.

00:30:45.660 --> 00:30:50.740
Little infant brains, you know, newborn brains
... A newborn brain is not like a miniature

00:30:50.740 --> 00:30:51.960
adult brain.

00:30:51.960 --> 00:31:00.780
It's not completely ... it's wiring isn't
completely finished and we ... So, what infants

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:06.530
are doing to some extent, is they're waiting
for a set of wiring instructions from the

00:31:06.530 --> 00:31:07.530
world.

00:31:07.530 --> 00:31:12.590
The brain expects certain inputs in order
for it to wire itself normally and it wires

00:31:12.590 --> 00:31:17.960
itself both to the physical circumstances
it grows up in, but also to social circumstances

00:31:17.960 --> 00:31:19.340
it grows up in.

00:31:19.340 --> 00:31:25.660
We encourage ... So, that's sort of the normal
aspect of brain development that's related

00:31:25.660 --> 00:31:31.340
to being wired for belief, but we also wire
our children for belief in other ways.

00:31:31.340 --> 00:31:32.850
We indulge them.

00:31:32.850 --> 00:31:39.650
In our culture, we indulge them in believing
in animacy of their blankets and their little

00:31:39.650 --> 00:31:45.150
cars and their little toys and some people
in this room might believe that their cars

00:31:45.150 --> 00:31:47.770
have minds, right?

00:31:47.770 --> 00:31:55.809
So, we do ... that's another way in which
brains can become wired for belief in the

00:31:55.809 --> 00:32:04.630
sense of development actually influences the
wiring of the brain.

00:32:04.630 --> 00:32:09.070
Then there, we could also talk about feelings
as the root of belief.

00:32:09.070 --> 00:32:13.840
That to some extent feeling is believing.

00:32:13.840 --> 00:32:18.809
When you believe ... When you feel something
very strongly, you are more likely to believe

00:32:18.809 --> 00:32:28.490
it and feeling is at the core of the wiring
of our brains, and really you could argue

00:32:28.490 --> 00:32:30.590
most mammalian brains.

00:32:30.590 --> 00:32:33.740
Some people would like to make that argument,
pull that argument even earlier.

00:32:33.740 --> 00:32:39.059
I'm going to come back to that in a for Zoran,
you've spent some time studying the human

00:32:39.059 --> 00:32:41.000
brain.

00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:47.100
Do you feel that there's evidence that we're
... there's an internal physiological predilection

00:32:47.100 --> 00:32:48.590
for religious belief?

00:32:48.590 --> 00:32:52.550
Yeah, I wouldn't so much ... Yes.

00:32:52.550 --> 00:32:58.559
As much as brain is I think organized to be
conscious, it's organized for spiritual experiences

00:32:58.559 --> 00:33:06.410
and indirectly for beliefs just as Steve and
Lisa pointed out.

00:33:06.410 --> 00:33:10.039
I think something happened to us, to our species,
right?

00:33:10.039 --> 00:33:14.370
We don't know when, maybe 3000 years ago,
5000 years, maybe longer.

00:33:14.370 --> 00:33:17.200
Suddenly, we became conscious.

00:33:17.200 --> 00:33:19.200
We became conscious in very unique way.

00:33:19.200 --> 00:33:25.040
It's not just we have experience, or that
we have conscious experience, but we know

00:33:25.040 --> 00:33:26.600
that we are conscious.

00:33:26.600 --> 00:33:29.250
We have implicit knowing that we are conscious.

00:33:29.250 --> 00:33:34.650
We have an expression of religiosity going
as far as we have records, you know, around

00:33:34.650 --> 00:33:40.220
3000 years ago, maybe longer actually, but
we don't have records any more of that, that

00:33:40.220 --> 00:33:43.870
people were really trying to figure out what
is this thing.

00:33:43.870 --> 00:33:47.039
We're conscious, what is it?

00:33:47.039 --> 00:33:48.540
Who is this person who is conscious?

00:33:48.540 --> 00:33:50.350
What is it that's conscious inside us.

00:33:50.350 --> 00:33:52.270
And also, what is this universe?

00:33:52.270 --> 00:33:58.100
The way it appears when we perceive it with
the depth of our consciousness, not just with

00:33:58.100 --> 00:34:01.419
the surface of our mind, but with the deepest
part of ourselves.

00:34:01.419 --> 00:34:08.369
And so that gives rise to some very kind of
a deep core sort of explorations in the nature

00:34:08.369 --> 00:34:12.090
of human mind that we have records of.

00:34:12.090 --> 00:34:21.179
When we look at the ... What I personally
feel is sort of the innermost core of the

00:34:21.179 --> 00:34:29.320
religious practices, pretty much in every
religious traditions we find this, this unitary

00:34:29.320 --> 00:34:33.970
experiences, experiences of consciousness
itself.

00:34:33.970 --> 00:34:40.619
They can be either very deep mental silence
in which all mental processes quiet down and

00:34:40.619 --> 00:34:47.300
then there is either just complete blackness
and then within it, there's just awareness.

00:34:47.300 --> 00:34:49.109
Consciousness itself.

00:34:49.109 --> 00:34:54.379
Doesn't think, doesn't feel, doesn't need
to do anything, but it's aware and knows that

00:34:54.379 --> 00:34:57.410
it's conscious innately, directly.

00:34:57.410 --> 00:34:58.410
Doesn't have to think.

00:34:58.410 --> 00:35:00.450
Doesn't have to take itself as an object.

00:35:00.450 --> 00:35:03.599
Just consciousness itself.

00:35:03.599 --> 00:35:12.480
Then, that deepest part of ourselves, if it
wakes up suddenly, and within our experience,

00:35:12.480 --> 00:35:18.680
then the quality of our experience changes
dramatically, from this ordinary experience

00:35:18.680 --> 00:35:23.950
where I'm over here, I am limited to my body,
I'm limited to my surface of my skin, whatever

00:35:23.950 --> 00:35:29.431
my mind has constructed and learned over the
course of my life, who I am, what the world

00:35:29.431 --> 00:35:33.220
is, how to relate to each other, how I relate
to others.

00:35:33.220 --> 00:35:38.440
So, we have this elaborate self-world model
inside our head that filters everything you

00:35:38.440 --> 00:35:39.859
experience.

00:35:39.859 --> 00:35:45.360
That takes a break temporarily, however briefly,
and suddenly we experience that everything

00:35:45.360 --> 00:35:48.220
is one reality.

00:35:48.220 --> 00:35:54.130
One interdependent, but also at the same time,
one consciousness that seems to extend, that's

00:35:54.130 --> 00:35:58.380
the experience and encompasses everything.

00:35:58.380 --> 00:36:03.570
I think that religiosity tried to capture
what this is.

00:36:03.570 --> 00:36:12.090
When the theistic religion says that God is
simultaneously transcendent and immanent in

00:36:12.090 --> 00:36:13.090
all things.

00:36:13.090 --> 00:36:14.710
So, in all things.

00:36:14.710 --> 00:36:20.120
In this experience here that we're having,
right now sitting in this wonderful place,

00:36:20.120 --> 00:36:24.310
this is the experience of God being transcendent
and immanent at the same time.

00:36:24.310 --> 00:36:26.460
That is one way of saying it, right?

00:36:26.460 --> 00:36:29.040
I didn't look at it that way, but that's very
true.

00:36:29.040 --> 00:36:33.030
So, another way to say it is that we have
two sides to our consciousness.

00:36:33.030 --> 00:36:35.740
One side is mind that creates experience.

00:36:35.740 --> 00:36:38.390
The other side is awareness which is just
like a mirror.

00:36:38.390 --> 00:36:42.610
It simply register what is happening without
doing anything to it.

00:36:42.610 --> 00:36:45.950
The two are different.

00:36:45.950 --> 00:36:51.760
In this view, they're separated by the substrate
which is kind of like an unconscious film.

00:36:51.760 --> 00:36:52.840
Matrix.

00:36:52.840 --> 00:36:56.820
It actually exists in the universe, they say.

00:36:56.820 --> 00:36:57.940
I don't.

00:36:57.940 --> 00:37:04.370
What's interesting, what happens is when the
mind wants to find what consciousness is,

00:37:04.370 --> 00:37:05.410
it just finds itself.

00:37:05.410 --> 00:37:11.860
It finds attention, it finds intelligence,
and it finds vigilance, but it can't ... If

00:37:11.860 --> 00:37:16.140
it doesn't know how, it can't penetrate through
this unconscious substrate.

00:37:16.140 --> 00:37:19.119
And then it's basically concludes there is
no consciousness.

00:37:19.119 --> 00:37:23.210
It's just a mental processes, right?

00:37:23.210 --> 00:37:27.830
From the side of awareness, what the substrate
does is awareness can't recognize itself.

00:37:27.830 --> 00:37:34.370
It can't recognize what it is directly so
it experiences itself as a subject who is

00:37:34.370 --> 00:37:36.920
having experience.

00:37:36.920 --> 00:37:43.540
From that perspective, spirituality and spiritual
beliefs are consciousness trying to find itself.

00:37:43.540 --> 00:37:45.990
It's trying to figure out what it is.

00:37:45.990 --> 00:37:54.970
So, Barbara, can you take us back to the earliest
evidence that we have for that kind of internal

00:37:54.970 --> 00:37:59.700
self-reflection that ultimately we think may
have been the seeds for religiosity?

00:37:59.700 --> 00:38:04.290
The first thing I'd like to start and say
is that, yes, it's certainly true that we

00:38:04.290 --> 00:38:09.540
attribute intentionality to a lot of animals,
but the fact also is that they are intentional.

00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:15.290
So, we certainly don't have a corner on the
market of intentionality or consciousness

00:38:15.290 --> 00:38:18.010
or sentience or any of these other things.

00:38:18.010 --> 00:38:22.820
But if we're going to talk about the human
evolutionary trajectory, we know that our

00:38:22.820 --> 00:38:26.020
species is about 200,000 years old.

00:38:26.020 --> 00:38:30.200
Our genus is around 2.4 million years old.

00:38:30.200 --> 00:38:35.910
So the question becomes when do we start seeing
any of these symptoms, if you will.

00:38:35.910 --> 00:38:41.220
It's very interesting that there's a cave
in South Africa, Rising Star cave, that is

00:38:41.220 --> 00:38:48.480
the home of this human, perhaps ancestor,
but hominid in any case, called Homo naledi

00:38:48.480 --> 00:38:54.180
and apparently there were numbers of individuals
who were literally dragged by others into

00:38:54.180 --> 00:38:58.390
a very deep, subterranean chamber in this
cave.

00:38:58.390 --> 00:39:03.640
So, Rising Star is a very famous project in
paleo anthropology and one can watch often

00:39:03.640 --> 00:39:08.800
live feeds of the scientists trying to study
these chambers and they have to crawl through

00:39:08.800 --> 00:39:12.630
these incredibly small passageways.

00:39:12.630 --> 00:39:19.070
And yet, we know that approximately 250,000
years ago, people were disposing of their

00:39:19.070 --> 00:39:25.490
dead in very intentional ritual ways, going
through a lot of effort and a lot of energy

00:39:25.490 --> 00:39:26.630
to do this.

00:39:26.630 --> 00:39:30.450
The problem becomes-
Is that controversial or is that why?

00:39:30.450 --> 00:39:36.500
Beside the fact that they're bringing the
people to the chamber is not particularly

00:39:36.500 --> 00:39:37.500
controversial.

00:39:37.500 --> 00:39:38.500
The next step…

00:39:38.500 --> 00:39:39.500
But whether it was a ritual burial.

00:39:39.500 --> 00:39:40.500
Exactly.

00:39:40.500 --> 00:39:43.380
The next step is controversial because of
course we have this small problem, which is

00:39:43.380 --> 00:39:49.520
that belief doesn't fossilize, so we don't
know, and we have people, we have a chamber,

00:39:49.520 --> 00:39:53.820
and we have our minds and as we're talking
about we're searching and yearning always

00:39:53.820 --> 00:39:55.099
to figure this out.

00:39:55.099 --> 00:40:00.320
But isn't it the case though that it's ... the
people of ... anthropologists have already

00:40:00.320 --> 00:40:05.869
discovered, let's say Homo neanderthals skeletons
that are ... You know, they've been buried

00:40:05.869 --> 00:40:09.359
and posed in a particular way with things
around them and so, it's-

00:40:09.359 --> 00:40:10.800
Like in Sungir, right?

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:15.609
Right, but we're going in a kind of order
so I'm starting a little earlier than Neanderthals.

00:40:15.609 --> 00:40:20.520
We have the roots of Neanderthal populations
this time, but what's so fascinating is that

00:40:20.520 --> 00:40:25.600
the Neanderthal burials don't come to a hundred
thousand years ago or 60,000 years ago in

00:40:25.600 --> 00:40:30.800
Sungir in Russia, which is a Homo sapiens
site just mentioned, is like 27,000.

00:40:30.800 --> 00:40:38.670
So, my idea is that, again, we have some glimmers
and some intriguing hints, 250,000 years ago.

00:40:38.670 --> 00:40:43.500
Now, let's just fast forward, let me leap
over many thousands of years, we've come to

00:40:43.500 --> 00:40:44.500
Neanderthals.

00:40:44.500 --> 00:40:46.700
They are not our ancestors.

00:40:46.700 --> 00:40:48.470
They are our cousins.

00:40:48.470 --> 00:40:54.609
We used to say that they lived from 200 something
thousand to 40,000 and then they went extinct.

00:40:54.609 --> 00:40:58.490
We no longer say that because here in the
audience there's tons of Neanderthal genetic

00:40:58.490 --> 00:41:04.310
material and many populations, except some
populations in Africa because we did not have

00:41:04.310 --> 00:41:11.270
Neanderthals in Africa, we find just as you
were saying, Lisa, that there are very intentional

00:41:11.270 --> 00:41:14.300
burials with all kinds of grave goods.

00:41:14.300 --> 00:41:17.630
So people didn't just stick people in the
earth.

00:41:17.630 --> 00:41:20.440
They marked the graves as something special.

00:41:20.440 --> 00:41:25.140
To give you one example, there's a 40,000
year old burial of a toddler in what's today

00:41:25.140 --> 00:41:32.980
Spain, with a hearth all around, 60 sets of
oryx and bison horns, a rhino skull.

00:41:32.980 --> 00:41:35.000
This was a place that mattered.

00:41:35.000 --> 00:41:38.619
In some sense we can think of it as a sacred
place.

00:41:38.619 --> 00:41:41.880
The question is, is there belief in an afterlife?

00:41:41.880 --> 00:41:44.690
Is there belief in supernatural beings?

00:41:44.690 --> 00:41:45.780
How would we know?

00:41:45.780 --> 00:41:49.119
We're imposing a great deal of our framework
onto the past.

00:41:49.119 --> 00:41:52.160
Keep going in time, we come to cave art.

00:41:52.160 --> 00:41:54.630
And, of course, we're familiar with the cave
paintings.

00:41:54.630 --> 00:42:00.840
These are not only early Homo sapiens, but
also in some cases Neanderthals.

00:42:00.840 --> 00:42:02.220
We do know that now.

00:42:02.220 --> 00:42:07.520
This is a relatively recent discovery, that
we are not the only cave painters, but what's

00:42:07.520 --> 00:42:12.180
fascinating for me about this is you have
these glorious depictions of animals that

00:42:12.180 --> 00:42:18.600
these people hunted, but in addition to that,
some very mystical and fantastic figures.

00:42:18.600 --> 00:42:21.150
A bird-headed man in Lascaux cave in France.

00:42:21.150 --> 00:42:24.250
A human that is part bison.

00:42:24.250 --> 00:42:26.310
Some other just wild figures.

00:42:26.310 --> 00:42:31.470
So this is not just people representing the
reality they saw before them, but rather there's

00:42:31.470 --> 00:42:37.260
an interest in what is not in front of you,
what is not just here and now.

00:42:37.260 --> 00:42:39.270
We fast forward one more time.

00:42:39.270 --> 00:42:47.450
We go to Turkey, to this particular, perhaps
temple, Gobekli Tepe, is dated to that period

00:42:47.450 --> 00:42:49.360
on a hill in Turkey.

00:42:49.360 --> 00:42:56.810
Massive 50 ton blocks that people moved onto
a hillside and carved with, again, elaborate,

00:42:56.810 --> 00:42:58.990
largely animal, images.

00:42:58.990 --> 00:43:01.460
We think this is a ritual space.

00:43:01.460 --> 00:43:02.460
Not everyone agrees.

00:43:02.460 --> 00:43:03.900
This is contentious.

00:43:03.900 --> 00:43:10.060
But in every single case there is a good argument
to be made for the possibility of the human

00:43:10.060 --> 00:43:15.570
brain uncoupling itself from the here and
now, to think about these questions of the

00:43:15.570 --> 00:43:17.160
supernatural.

00:43:17.160 --> 00:43:19.630
And we have hints.

00:43:19.630 --> 00:43:25.720
We have to go forward in time again before
we come to a really institutionalized religious

00:43:25.720 --> 00:43:26.720
system.

00:43:26.720 --> 00:43:31.030
But again, the reason that I think the human
religious imagination evolved because of all

00:43:31.030 --> 00:43:32.030
these earlier cues.

00:43:32.030 --> 00:43:33.030
Right, right.

00:43:33.030 --> 00:43:37.530
I think it's important ... I think that Barbara's
bringing up something really important and

00:43:37.530 --> 00:43:43.670
that is, we're all talking ... We're sort
of fluidly talking back and forth as if spirituality

00:43:43.670 --> 00:43:49.880
and religiosity are identical forms of meaning
making and they're really not.

00:43:49.880 --> 00:43:53.040
There are many, many ways to be spiritual.

00:43:53.040 --> 00:43:59.190
Some involve belief in a supernatural deity
with agency, but not all of them do, right?

00:43:59.190 --> 00:44:06.880
Some of them ... sometimes spirituality means
just being full of awe and wonder at something

00:44:06.880 --> 00:44:12.970
larger than you, that transcends itself transcendence,
like in connecting with nature for example.

00:44:12.970 --> 00:44:15.060
As Einstein was saying in his book.

00:44:15.060 --> 00:44:20.820
Exactly, and I think ... So, one way to think
about this is that when we're talking about

00:44:20.820 --> 00:44:27.510
the evolution of religious thought or spiritual
thought or we're talking about the biology

00:44:27.510 --> 00:44:34.010
of spiritual thought, we have to be thinking
about the fact that we're talking about different

00:44:34.010 --> 00:44:35.380
psychological features here.

00:44:35.380 --> 00:44:39.980
One has to do with connecting to something
in the moment that's bigger than you and that

00:44:39.980 --> 00:44:40.980
might transcend you.

00:44:40.980 --> 00:44:47.940
One element or feature is about explanation,
right?

00:44:47.940 --> 00:44:49.740
Another is about agency.

00:44:49.740 --> 00:44:57.730
And so those may not have all evolved at the
same time, or perhaps they're not all meaningful

00:44:57.730 --> 00:45:02.619
for all people, so maybe everyone in this
room has had a spiritual experience.

00:45:02.619 --> 00:45:07.080
They might not call it that, but they've had
an experience where they've connected to something

00:45:07.080 --> 00:45:12.020
that's bigger than themselves that leaves
them feeling awestruck, but not everyone would

00:45:12.020 --> 00:45:17.450
take the additional steps of trying to find
an explanation in that or trying to find agency

00:45:17.450 --> 00:45:18.551
in that and so forth.

00:45:18.551 --> 00:45:26.180
But if we do go and focus on beliefs that
do transcend just a sense that there's a larger

00:45:26.180 --> 00:45:32.550
reality that you were a part of and goes toward
a supernatural belief in things that science

00:45:32.550 --> 00:45:39.910
typically would not confirm, do you see the
potential for an adaptive value, for a progression

00:45:39.910 --> 00:45:45.230
that would lead to a brain that would have
a tendency to do that?

00:45:45.230 --> 00:45:52.990
It's hard to give an adaptive explanation
for belief in entities that don't exist.

00:45:52.990 --> 00:45:59.900
There can be an adapter of explanation for
the search for explanations which obviously

00:45:59.900 --> 00:46:08.370
are not infallible and they can be misled
by absence of evidence, by people who have

00:46:08.370 --> 00:46:11.740
an interest in promulgating certain explanations.

00:46:11.740 --> 00:46:20.250
I think what you have to direct the question
at not the content of beliefs that we associate

00:46:20.250 --> 00:46:25.800
with particular religions, but just in particular
ways of thinking, but ways of interpreting

00:46:25.800 --> 00:46:32.020
the world, ways in which people influence
the beliefs of one another.

00:46:32.020 --> 00:46:37.540
What are the kinds of things that we can hypothesize
and then what does that leave us vulnerable

00:46:37.540 --> 00:46:43.751
to hypothesizing which, from the perspective
of science, we know may be incorrect, but

00:46:43.751 --> 00:46:50.310
can nonetheless be very seductive to a mind
that is apt to think in certain directions.

00:46:50.310 --> 00:46:56.320
So what's your view say of those who've made
the case that the adaptive value is not so

00:46:56.320 --> 00:47:04.339
much in the actual belief in things that perhaps
don't exist, but it is from the cohesion,

00:47:04.339 --> 00:47:09.810
the group cohesion that that can yield if
there are many people for whom that belief

00:47:09.810 --> 00:47:13.310
is shared, then all of a sudden you've got
a stronger group bonding?

00:47:13.310 --> 00:47:17.750
Does that hold any weight for you at all?

00:47:17.750 --> 00:47:27.670
There is a folk theory of evolution that adaptations
are all for group cohesion because whenever

00:47:27.670 --> 00:47:34.370
there is some mysterious aspect of human psychology
for which it's not clear what the adaptive

00:47:34.370 --> 00:47:37.180
value is, people will say, "Well, it fosters
group cohesion."

00:47:37.180 --> 00:47:38.640
Why do we enjoy music?

00:47:38.640 --> 00:47:39.640
Group cohesion.

00:47:39.640 --> 00:47:40.640
Why do we dance?

00:47:40.640 --> 00:47:41.640
Group cohesion.

00:47:41.640 --> 00:47:43.460
But there a couple things wrong with that
style of explanation.

00:47:43.460 --> 00:47:46.950
I'm very deeply suspicious of the explanation
always says group cohesion.

00:47:46.950 --> 00:47:52.760
One of them is, group cohesion is not, in
fact, what natural selection selects for.

00:47:52.760 --> 00:47:56.130
It selects for propagation of genes.

00:47:56.130 --> 00:48:00.650
Sometimes groups, cohesive groups can help
the individuals that compose those groups,

00:48:00.650 --> 00:48:04.740
but if a group is too cohesive, you could
be exploited by the group.

00:48:04.740 --> 00:48:05.859
You could be cannon fodder.

00:48:05.859 --> 00:48:09.480
You could be a sacrificial victim for the
benefit, for the cohesion of the group.

00:48:09.480 --> 00:48:14.850
But any gene that would allow you to be exploited
by the group would be selected out because

00:48:14.850 --> 00:48:17.359
genes are selected much more quickly than
groups.

00:48:17.359 --> 00:48:25.490
Also, I think it's too easy to use our own
intuition that we like to bond over music,

00:48:25.490 --> 00:48:31.790
over religion and so on, but that is itself
a part of our psychology that needs an explanation.

00:48:31.790 --> 00:48:36.970
Why would beliefs in invisible entities make
a group coherent more?

00:48:36.970 --> 00:48:39.360
You can't take that for granted.

00:48:39.360 --> 00:48:41.970
That's as much of a puzzle to a psychologist
as…

00:48:41.970 --> 00:48:46.250
But we do see, we do see evidence of that
even though we may need to explain it.

00:48:46.250 --> 00:48:52.450
We do, although ... the supernatural beliefs
can also divide a group, needless to say.

00:48:52.450 --> 00:49:00.940
There are wars of religion and precisely because
they ... The content of those beliefs aren't

00:49:00.940 --> 00:49:03.010
derived from shared experience.

00:49:03.010 --> 00:49:05.190
They're not things that everyone can just
open their eyes and see.

00:49:05.190 --> 00:49:06.359
They're things you have to be told.

00:49:06.359 --> 00:49:11.050
And that means that if you're told by different
shamans or different priests or imams than

00:49:11.050 --> 00:49:12.820
you can go to war over those beliefs.

00:49:12.820 --> 00:49:17.240
That's why I think that group cohesion doesn't
strike me as a satisfying explanation for

00:49:17.240 --> 00:49:18.240
belief.

00:49:18.240 --> 00:49:19.240
Lisa, do you have a different view of that?

00:49:19.240 --> 00:49:23.550
I have ... yes, I think I have a different
view or maybe I want to add some information.

00:49:23.550 --> 00:49:24.550
You can be contentious.

00:49:24.550 --> 00:49:27.829
You could just like-
Believe me, I have no problem with being contentious,

00:49:27.829 --> 00:49:28.839
at all.

00:49:28.839 --> 00:49:31.920
Anyone who knows me, knows this is true.

00:49:31.920 --> 00:49:41.550
Here's what I want to say, that I think that
there is an immediate advantage potentially,

00:49:41.550 --> 00:49:51.180
which is there are two that I can think of
that relate to the functioning of a nervous

00:49:51.180 --> 00:49:53.089
system in the following way.

00:49:53.089 --> 00:49:59.579
First of all, uncertainty is tremendously
stressful for a human nervous system.

00:49:59.579 --> 00:50:01.859
And I don't mean stress in a euphemistic way.

00:50:01.859 --> 00:50:11.060
I mean it adds a metabolic burden to a nervous
system which if it persists can actually make

00:50:11.060 --> 00:50:18.830
someone sick and I think religious beliefs
can reduce uncertainty.

00:50:18.830 --> 00:50:25.710
They sometimes explain the unexplainable.

00:50:25.710 --> 00:50:33.280
Things that we now might explain through science,
used to be thought of as magic or as caused

00:50:33.280 --> 00:50:34.280
by a deity.

00:50:34.280 --> 00:50:41.599
So, I think in some ways it is not just psychologically
comforting, it's actually physiologically

00:50:41.599 --> 00:50:44.530
potentially less ... it reduces people's stress.

00:50:44.530 --> 00:50:51.040
It reduces their, what scientists would call
allostatic burden.

00:50:51.040 --> 00:50:57.869
Very simply, just step back one minute and
say, partly our brains evolved not to think

00:50:57.869 --> 00:51:02.530
and see and feel, but in order to regulate
the systems of our body.

00:51:02.530 --> 00:51:05.480
As our bodies got more complex, brains got
bigger.

00:51:05.480 --> 00:51:14.579
A brain's main job is to keep the systems
of your body alive and well so that you can

00:51:14.579 --> 00:51:19.290
propagate your genes to the next ... let me
just finish.

00:51:19.290 --> 00:51:28.220
I know you're going to disagree, but ...
Your brain is constantly running a budget

00:51:28.220 --> 00:51:37.450
for the resources in your body and it's not
budgeting money, it's budgeting glucose and

00:51:37.450 --> 00:51:38.750
salt and so on and so forth.

00:51:38.750 --> 00:51:43.420
And so, if you think about your brain running
a budget for your body, uncertainty just drains

00:51:43.420 --> 00:51:44.470
that budget.

00:51:44.470 --> 00:51:49.240
Drains that budget much faster and makes it
really harder for people.

00:51:49.240 --> 00:51:54.130
There's also, I think, a social aspect to
this too, in the sense that we are social

00:51:54.130 --> 00:51:55.990
animals, we evolved to be social animals.

00:51:55.990 --> 00:51:58.740
It's one of our major adaptive advantages,
to be social animals.

00:51:58.740 --> 00:52:02.940
But what that means is that we regulate each
other's nervous systems.

00:52:02.940 --> 00:52:05.590
We don't bear that body budget on our own.

00:52:05.590 --> 00:52:10.480
We have other people to help us do it.

00:52:10.480 --> 00:52:12.470
There are other social species, right?

00:52:12.470 --> 00:52:17.109
So, insects are social, and they regulate
each other's nervous systems through chemicals,

00:52:17.109 --> 00:52:18.640
through scent.

00:52:18.640 --> 00:52:26.930
Rats, and some mammals, add touch and they
might add hearing and primates add vision.

00:52:26.930 --> 00:52:30.770
We, as primates, have all of those ways to
regulate each other.

00:52:30.770 --> 00:52:34.950
Plus, we have ideas that we share.

00:52:34.950 --> 00:52:41.710
And so, there are many ways in which religious
belief can actually reduce the metabolic burden

00:52:41.710 --> 00:52:42.710
on the nervous system.

00:52:42.710 --> 00:52:43.710
Is there data for that?

00:52:43.710 --> 00:52:46.790
I mean, is there data that really makes a
convincing argument that religious belief

00:52:46.790 --> 00:52:49.770
does reduce ...
There actually is.

00:52:49.770 --> 00:52:53.650
I'm not advocating this, I'm just saying as
a scientist, there is data.

00:52:53.650 --> 00:53:01.089
There are data to show that people who ... I
want to say this, you know, I'm not negating

00:53:01.089 --> 00:53:09.190
any of the challenges or problems that religious
belief introduces to a fitness argument.

00:53:09.190 --> 00:53:14.910
I'm just saying that there is this other side
where there are data to show that people who

00:53:14.910 --> 00:53:24.750
are religious actually are somewhat happier
and healthier and have greater well-being.

00:53:24.750 --> 00:53:27.760
But that's because, of course, they're living
amongst other people who believe what they

00:53:27.760 --> 00:53:28.760
believe.

00:53:28.760 --> 00:53:31.750
Steve, you had a response again.

00:53:31.750 --> 00:53:34.599
Barbara ... yeah.

00:53:34.599 --> 00:53:40.250
I just really wanted to make the point that
we're actually operating in a framework of

00:53:40.250 --> 00:53:46.540
human exceptionalism when we keep asking things
like, was social cohesion part of the reason

00:53:46.540 --> 00:53:50.360
that we were religious, or did being religious
drive social cohesion, because, you know ... Why

00:53:50.360 --> 00:53:52.740
are we not asking about orcas, for example.

00:53:52.740 --> 00:53:58.980
Orcas are exquisitely cohesive and they do
things as a group and they regulate each other

00:53:58.980 --> 00:54:03.240
as individuals and they solve their problems
as a group and they manage to do that without

00:54:03.240 --> 00:54:04.240
God.

00:54:04.240 --> 00:54:06.290
And chimpanzees manage to do this without
God.

00:54:06.290 --> 00:54:11.869
So, I think that in addition to the problems
that Steven pointed out with social cohesion

00:54:11.869 --> 00:54:17.970
arguments, that they're thrown out constantly
that we can just look at the natural world

00:54:17.970 --> 00:54:21.070
and we can see that there are so many different
pathways for this.

00:54:21.070 --> 00:54:25.280
If we only look at our species and we don't
take this comparative approach.

00:54:25.280 --> 00:54:27.270
We're not going to get answers to these questions.

00:54:27.270 --> 00:54:28.270
Yeah.

00:54:28.270 --> 00:54:29.270
Steve.

00:54:29.270 --> 00:54:33.990
Lisa, I agree that are religious belief can
reduce stress but I don't think that that

00:54:33.990 --> 00:54:37.210
can be an explanation as to why it's adaptive.

00:54:37.210 --> 00:54:41.791
Because the fact that uncertainty leads to
stress is itself an adaptation, namely there

00:54:41.791 --> 00:54:47.109
... we're missing some information that's
critical to our well-being and we're ... when

00:54:47.109 --> 00:54:53.240
we get stressed and nervous that motivates
us to seek out that information or to act

00:54:53.240 --> 00:54:57.150
in a way that keeps us safe even in the state
of ignorance.

00:54:57.150 --> 00:55:03.750
But there can't be an adaptation to reduce
stress by false certainty.

00:55:03.750 --> 00:55:08.069
That is, by being certain about something,
some claim about the world that in fact is

00:55:08.069 --> 00:55:09.069
not true.

00:55:09.069 --> 00:55:14.490
Because if I'm really nervous, say because
I think there might be a predator, and someone

00:55:14.490 --> 00:55:20.640
convinces me, no, it's actually a rabbit appearing
in the guise of a predatory cat, that might

00:55:20.640 --> 00:55:22.720
reduce my stress, but it's not an adaptation.

00:55:22.720 --> 00:55:23.720
Fair enough, fair enough.

00:55:23.720 --> 00:55:28.340
So Barbara, you gave us some history of where
you think this may have begun all the way

00:55:28.340 --> 00:55:31.780
back in human history.

00:55:31.780 --> 00:55:35.670
What's your sense of why it has persisted
for so long?

00:55:35.670 --> 00:55:39.450
I tend to come back to this issue of community
and practice.

00:55:39.450 --> 00:55:45.010
Because I think if we shift the perspective
from looking so much at belief and sacred

00:55:45.010 --> 00:55:47.660
texts, which we tend to do in today's world.

00:55:47.660 --> 00:55:52.839
You know, you put up a slide that talked about
the percentage of the pie in terms of Christianity

00:55:52.839 --> 00:55:59.400
and Islam and that's one important aspect
of this, but I think that for many of the

00:55:59.400 --> 00:56:05.110
world's people, there is just something that's
irreplaceable about that sense of community,

00:56:05.110 --> 00:56:07.450
that sense of ritual practice and that sense
of familiarity.

00:56:07.450 --> 00:56:13.720
And it is ... Sure, it's possible to try to
replace that with some other ways to find

00:56:13.720 --> 00:56:18.350
those same things, but there is something
about the connectivity that comes through

00:56:18.350 --> 00:56:20.640
the transcendence that I think is important.

00:56:20.640 --> 00:56:25.060
When you bring those two elements together,
the community and the transcendence and sharing

00:56:25.060 --> 00:56:26.530
that emotional meaning making.

00:56:26.530 --> 00:56:31.390
And one of the things that i like very much
about the people who are discussing whether

00:56:31.390 --> 00:56:37.520
there's faith in other animals is the idea
of breaking the link between making religion

00:56:37.520 --> 00:56:39.960
always be about text and belief.

00:56:39.960 --> 00:56:45.020
So I think that that helps us understand this
question a little bit.

00:56:45.020 --> 00:56:51.109
The idea ... I think about what Martin Buber
wrote, coming from the tradition of Judaism,

00:56:51.109 --> 00:56:54.350
when he wrote that all of real life is encounter.

00:56:54.350 --> 00:56:59.740
There's something that's particularly transporting
about sharing encounters of transcendence

00:56:59.740 --> 00:57:04.230
and I really feel it has something to do with
the persistence that we see.

00:57:04.230 --> 00:57:09.630
But clearly, when we talk about the spirituality
instinct, that's a very fraught term because

00:57:09.630 --> 00:57:12.870
of the secularization that's happening in
the world.

00:57:12.870 --> 00:57:18.690
If that really were to be considered an instinct,
how do we explain the tremendous transformation

00:57:18.690 --> 00:57:20.180
that we're undergoing?

00:57:20.180 --> 00:57:25.300
So people are finding humanism communities,
other communities with a different type of

00:57:25.300 --> 00:57:26.830
transcendent connection.

00:57:26.830 --> 00:57:32.640
I think there's a balance between what continues
as very, very strong tradition that carries

00:57:32.640 --> 00:57:37.920
communities forward together with new ways
of imagining some of these very same things

00:57:37.920 --> 00:57:39.310
that are coming about.

00:57:39.310 --> 00:57:42.280
The ways that people can experience religion
now.

00:57:42.280 --> 00:57:45.220
I mean, they extend into communities with
AI.

00:57:45.220 --> 00:57:50.780
They extend to virtual realities, virtual
churches, virtual connection, virtual mosques,

00:57:50.780 --> 00:57:57.369
but also the idea that we're beginning to
think just differently about animals and nature.

00:57:57.369 --> 00:57:59.670
We know Emily Dickinson's church, right?

00:57:59.670 --> 00:58:04.530
Well, we also know that one of the beauties
of the evolutionary perspective among many

00:58:04.530 --> 00:58:09.160
others, is not only understanding our own
place in the world, but our really deep sharing

00:58:09.160 --> 00:58:10.160
with other animals.

00:58:10.160 --> 00:58:15.120
And so, I think there's the possibility that
we are going to continue this shift of finding

00:58:15.120 --> 00:58:19.280
different ways of sharing transcendence as
I feel with nature, with animals.

00:58:19.280 --> 00:58:23.970
So does that transcendence relate to ... I
mean, Steven J. Gould, I believe once said

00:58:23.970 --> 00:58:28.510
that all religions begin with an awareness
of death.

00:58:28.510 --> 00:58:35.560
So, is that transcendence profoundly connected
with death or is it somehow independent of

00:58:35.560 --> 00:58:36.560
it?

00:58:36.560 --> 00:58:37.970
I think you've hit on an important thing.

00:58:37.970 --> 00:58:42.890
Part of my last six years of my work has been
very profoundly taken up with the question

00:58:42.890 --> 00:58:46.000
of animal grief and animal mourning.

00:58:46.000 --> 00:58:50.970
And I'm not suggesting, again, to be very
clear that animals have some kind of sacred

00:58:50.970 --> 00:58:57.520
sense of death, but they have a deep awareness
of loss, so that we find over and over again-

00:58:57.520 --> 00:58:58.520
Can you give an example?

00:58:58.520 --> 00:58:59.520
I mean, that's…

00:58:59.520 --> 00:59:01.430
Yeah, I can give loads of examples.

00:59:01.430 --> 00:59:07.230
For example, with elephants we know that the
entire community responds if a matriarch dies.

00:59:07.230 --> 00:59:10.470
There was one particular example in Africa.

00:59:10.470 --> 00:59:17.099
A particular community of scientists who followed
for seven days, a parade of mourners who came

00:59:17.099 --> 00:59:19.920
to this particular matriarch who had died.

00:59:19.920 --> 00:59:22.460
Her name was Eleanor.

00:59:22.460 --> 00:59:25.070
Not only her family, but matriarchs of other
families.

00:59:25.070 --> 00:59:29.020
Some stood vigil over the body, some rocked
over the body.

00:59:29.020 --> 00:59:30.579
Others showed distress.

00:59:30.579 --> 00:59:36.349
So my definition of animal grief involves
some kind of symptom of distress.

00:59:36.349 --> 00:59:41.270
Social withdrawal, failure to eat, failure
to sleep, some vocalizations.

00:59:41.270 --> 00:59:46.589
But it's not only the, what I call the usual
suspects, the big brained mammals like chimpanzees,

00:59:46.589 --> 00:59:49.109
cetaceans and elephants where we see this.

00:59:49.109 --> 00:59:55.180
My research is showing that we find it in
animals as different as collared peccaries

00:59:55.180 --> 01:00:02.180
in Arizona, chickens, all sorts of domestic
animals, the animals that we live with.

01:00:02.180 --> 01:00:08.510
And again, what I think is so important about
this is not necessarily that the animals have

01:00:08.510 --> 01:00:14.320
the same awareness of death that we have,
but that they feel this profound sense of

01:00:14.320 --> 01:00:15.750
loss.

01:00:15.750 --> 01:00:21.520
That is emotional meaning making and that's
where they enter into this community of sort

01:00:21.520 --> 01:00:26.500
of a transcendent experience in an animal
sort of way that I think is the foundation

01:00:26.500 --> 01:00:27.579
for this discussion.

01:00:27.579 --> 01:00:30.460
So, Steve, let me ask you.

01:00:30.460 --> 01:00:36.170
Transcendent experience, community, is one
powerful way of thinking about what religion

01:00:36.170 --> 01:00:37.660
provides.

01:00:37.660 --> 01:00:42.160
On the other side of the discussion, you've
got people like Dan Dennett.

01:00:42.160 --> 01:00:48.920
You got people like Pascal Boyer, and various
others, whose explanation tends more toward

01:00:48.920 --> 01:00:50.790
a mechanism.

01:00:50.790 --> 01:00:52.300
The spreading of ideas.

01:00:52.300 --> 01:00:54.180
The spreading of memes, you know?

01:00:54.180 --> 01:00:59.150
An idea jumps from brain to brain, to brain
and it naturally tickles certain receptors

01:00:59.150 --> 01:01:04.730
that we are naturally attuned to and therefore
certain ideas have a tendency to stick and

01:01:04.730 --> 01:01:09.950
spread, among them being the very ideas that
constitute religious belief.

01:01:09.950 --> 01:01:16.579
Is that an approach that you think gives us
insight or is that not a useful way of thinking

01:01:16.579 --> 01:01:17.579
about it?

01:01:17.579 --> 01:01:21.740
Yeah, because what puzzles us when we try
to explain the prevalence of religious belief,

01:01:21.740 --> 01:01:28.670
is not so much why people mourn the dead,
feel a sense of loss, feel it profoundly affects

01:01:28.670 --> 01:01:32.780
their lives because it does profoundly affect
their lives, it ought to.

01:01:32.780 --> 01:01:37.740
If you didn't mourn someone when they were
dead, when they die how could you have loved

01:01:37.740 --> 01:01:40.040
them when they were alive?

01:01:40.040 --> 01:01:46.890
That is, in a sense an easier set of reactions
to explain.

01:01:46.890 --> 01:01:52.640
What puzzles about religion is belief in the
Trinity and in hell and in 72 virgins and

01:01:52.640 --> 01:01:58.510
all of the other contentful beliefs that go
well beyond a sense of awe at the immensity

01:01:58.510 --> 01:02:01.280
of the cosmos or loss in the sense of death.

01:02:01.280 --> 01:02:09.530
That's where Pascal Boyer and Dennis Barbour
and others going to step in to why we're vulnerable

01:02:09.530 --> 01:02:19.980
to such specific beliefs as opposed to emotional
reactions to major events that affect us.

01:02:19.980 --> 01:02:27.309
There, I should actually credit Pascal Boyer
for linking the idea that we are mentalizing,

01:02:27.309 --> 01:02:32.609
we're apt to attribute minds to others as
one of the core explanations for why we are

01:02:32.609 --> 01:02:35.710
subject to religious beliefs that leads to
spiritualist beliefs.

01:02:35.710 --> 01:02:36.710
Right.

01:02:36.710 --> 01:02:43.530
So, Lisa, what's your view on these two sort
of poles, the need for community transcendent

01:02:43.530 --> 01:02:50.150
experience and perhaps something that just
speaks to the way in which certain ideas naturally

01:02:50.150 --> 01:03:00.720
stick inside a brain that evolved to perform
certain tasks and survive?

01:03:00.720 --> 01:03:08.670
I think that both of those explanations to
some extent are phenomena.

01:03:08.670 --> 01:03:13.181
I'm not really sure if you're referring to
them as explanations or just phenomena, are

01:03:13.181 --> 01:03:27.500
actually rooted in our sociality as a species,
so I think it's not a metaphor to say that

01:03:27.500 --> 01:03:28.850
we regulate each other.

01:03:28.850 --> 01:03:34.920
We do, in very substantial ways and in ways
that we're completely unaware of and part

01:03:34.920 --> 01:03:51.990
of how we do this is we create meaning that
is shared and realities that emerge only by

01:03:51.990 --> 01:03:55.390
virtue of collective agreement.

01:03:55.390 --> 01:04:02.319
What I mean by that is ... We're talking here,
for example, about grief and that animals,

01:04:02.319 --> 01:04:04.830
non-human animals feel grief and so on.

01:04:04.830 --> 01:04:09.760
Non-human animals feel loss, for sure.

01:04:09.760 --> 01:04:13.020
I think there's no question that that's the
case, and they suffer.

01:04:13.020 --> 01:04:20.400
I think there's no question that's the case,
but research on emotion suggests pretty clearly

01:04:20.400 --> 01:04:28.460
that there is no inherent emotional meaning
in any set of physical signals that occur

01:04:28.460 --> 01:04:30.310
from your body.

01:04:30.310 --> 01:04:37.609
What we do is ... humans, is we learn to impose
meanings on those signals, right?

01:04:37.609 --> 01:04:43.230
So, a scowling face for example, is not a
universal display of anger.

01:04:43.230 --> 01:04:48.250
People only scowl about 25% of the time when
they're angry and they scowl at many other

01:04:48.250 --> 01:04:53.150
times when they're not and there are many
cultures around the world, including hunter-gatherers

01:04:53.150 --> 01:04:57.869
who don't recognize a scowl as anger, for
example.

01:04:57.869 --> 01:05:02.520
In many cultures, and it's an interesting
question about why this is the case, but we'll

01:05:02.520 --> 01:05:10.350
just hold that aside for a moment, what we
do is we impose meaning on a scowling face,

01:05:10.350 --> 01:05:16.840
we impose meaning on a scowl and by virtue
of that meaning that we've imposed, the scowl

01:05:16.840 --> 01:05:22.160
actually literally takes on that meaning and
we can easily predict what's going to happen

01:05:22.160 --> 01:05:23.569
next.

01:05:23.569 --> 01:05:27.900
What I mean by this is it sort of works in
the same way as money works, right?

01:05:27.900 --> 01:05:35.530
There's no inherent ... Nothing that's ever
served as currency in human cultures does

01:05:35.530 --> 01:05:38.120
so by virtue of its physical nature alone.

01:05:38.120 --> 01:05:44.210
What happens is a group of humans impose a
meaning on pieces of paper, or little rocks,

01:05:44.210 --> 01:05:50.950
or salt, or barley, or big rocks in the ocean
that can't be moved, or mortgages, or any

01:05:50.950 --> 01:05:55.490
number of things and all of a sudden, those
things literally take on value.

01:05:55.490 --> 01:05:59.340
They can be traded for material goods only
because we all agree that they can and when

01:05:59.340 --> 01:06:04.740
someone moves their agreement, when people
withdraw some number, people withdraw their

01:06:04.740 --> 01:06:07.450
agreement, those things no longer have value.

01:06:07.450 --> 01:06:12.380
Well, emotions are kind of built in the same
way.

01:06:12.380 --> 01:06:20.450
Heart rates change, faces move, distress can
occur out of loss when you lose someone who

01:06:20.450 --> 01:06:26.380
helps to regulate your body budget and you
lose that person, you feel like you've lost

01:06:26.380 --> 01:06:32.990
a part of yourself because sort of you have
actually lost someone who's helped you regulate

01:06:32.990 --> 01:06:34.920
your nervous system.

01:06:34.920 --> 01:06:46.420
We impose meaning on those physical events
that take on that meaning.

01:06:46.420 --> 01:06:51.599
I mean the physical events take on those meanings
by virtue of the fact that we, as a culture,

01:06:51.599 --> 01:06:55.430
agree that that's the case.

01:06:55.430 --> 01:07:08.710
I think that in my view, this is partly why
memes occur, because ideas are contagious

01:07:08.710 --> 01:07:18.890
in a sense because we often as part of our
... one of our superpowers as a species is

01:07:18.890 --> 01:07:22.760
the ability to create meaning.

01:07:22.760 --> 01:07:28.660
The ability to create something real where
there used to be nothing real, only by virtue

01:07:28.660 --> 01:07:32.120
of collective agreement.

01:07:32.120 --> 01:07:36.680
We impose meaning on something physical and
then that physical thing takes on a bigger

01:07:36.680 --> 01:07:37.930
meaning.

01:07:37.930 --> 01:07:45.130
To some extent, I think we also do this with
what we think of as transcendent experiences.

01:07:45.130 --> 01:07:53.260
So, when a group of people are all together
having a similar experience at being awestruck

01:07:53.260 --> 01:08:02.099
or wonderstruck at something in nature, there's
an opportunity for creating social reality,

01:08:02.099 --> 01:08:09.250
for creating a meaning that wasn't there before,
that supersedes just the shared wonder of

01:08:09.250 --> 01:08:13.940
the moment and so, I don't see these ends
as really different.

01:08:13.940 --> 01:08:17.589
I see them as kind of emerging out of the
same capacities-

01:08:17.589 --> 01:08:26.250
So, in the remaining time, maybe we can just
focus on humans and on religious belief and

01:08:26.250 --> 01:08:28.319
maybe we could start with you, Barbara.

01:08:28.319 --> 01:08:29.569
Mm-hmm (affirmative).

01:08:29.569 --> 01:08:36.560
There's been a view that's been around for
a long time that as science progresses, it

01:08:36.560 --> 01:08:42.240
kind of pushes out the need for religion,
in terms of its explanatory capacities and

01:08:42.240 --> 01:08:43.240
so forth.

01:08:43.240 --> 01:08:48.859
Now it's suggested over time that the role
of religion would decrease.

01:08:48.859 --> 01:08:56.179
Do you imagine that that is the pattern that
will play out or is that a completely wrong

01:08:56.179 --> 01:09:00.009
and oblique way of thinking about the role
of religion and therefore what its future

01:09:00.009 --> 01:09:01.009
will be?

01:09:01.009 --> 01:09:02.009
Yeah, it's interesting.

01:09:02.009 --> 01:09:03.170
I feel two things at the same time.

01:09:03.170 --> 01:09:10.310
I do think that the increasing tendency towards
humanism and secularization is a very welcome

01:09:10.310 --> 01:09:11.310
thing.

01:09:11.310 --> 01:09:15.900
I mean, I did say that I am here speaking
as an atheist, as a person who is a non-believer.

01:09:15.900 --> 01:09:21.160
We certainly want to be able to think clearly
about science and about the forces that act

01:09:21.160 --> 01:09:27.160
in this world and we know, all of us know
that religion is not always helpful in that

01:09:27.160 --> 01:09:28.530
particular way.

01:09:28.530 --> 01:09:35.679
At the same time, I think it's really important
to think again about the cross-cultural patterns

01:09:35.679 --> 01:09:40.969
and the number of people in the world who
don't fall into believing in big sky gods,

01:09:40.969 --> 01:09:44.339
who don't even, in some cases, have a word
for religion.

01:09:44.339 --> 01:09:50.190
I'm not suggesting that that makes them different
in any kind of scale of intelligence, not

01:09:50.190 --> 01:09:51.190
at all.

01:09:51.190 --> 01:09:55.130
We know that all human populations have the
same capacities.

01:09:55.130 --> 01:09:58.910
But sometimes, just being religious is just
the way life is.

01:09:58.910 --> 01:10:03.969
It's so much a part of the era, of the way
that you live that it's not something that

01:10:03.969 --> 01:10:05.239
is going to change.

01:10:05.239 --> 01:10:11.260
I think these are two different ways of looking
at it and I'm not sure how to weigh them.

01:10:11.260 --> 01:10:13.650
I'd be interested to hear what other people
would say about that.

01:10:13.650 --> 01:10:14.650
Why don't we go right down the line.

01:10:14.650 --> 01:10:22.530
Zoran, do you have any ...
I think that at its best, it gives us a framework

01:10:22.530 --> 01:10:26.680
to experience the spirituality, to be able
to connection to something that's larger than

01:10:26.680 --> 01:10:28.590
ourselves.

01:10:28.590 --> 01:10:36.910
And so, if it fulfills that role for people,
I think then that's, you know, that it's its

01:10:36.910 --> 01:10:37.910
purpose.

01:10:37.910 --> 01:10:44.871
I hope that as the ages go, the science and
spirituality basically flow through each other

01:10:44.871 --> 01:10:48.240
smoothly.

01:10:48.240 --> 01:10:52.470
I think that for that, really we just need
more research in these topics.

01:10:52.470 --> 01:10:58.880
Lisa, thoughts on the future?

01:10:58.880 --> 01:11:03.300
I think I would stand by my descriptions that
I think that there are some advantages to

01:11:03.300 --> 01:11:07.820
religious belief, but I think there are also
some major, major disadvantages, some of which

01:11:07.820 --> 01:11:14.070
Steve has talked about and I think it ... From
my perspective it's probably about time to

01:11:14.070 --> 01:11:19.130
wonder whether or not the disadvantages outweigh
the advantages, frankly.

01:11:19.130 --> 01:11:23.420
Because there are other meaning making systems
that are available to humans to help them

01:11:23.420 --> 01:11:29.090
make sense of the world, some of which may
not have the disadvantages.

01:11:29.090 --> 01:11:33.800
They may have the advantages of religious
belief, but they might not also have the disadvantages.

01:11:33.800 --> 01:11:46.500
So, I probably lean more in the direction
of wondering how it would be possible to test

01:11:46.500 --> 01:11:48.840
that, to investigate that.

01:11:48.840 --> 01:11:51.120
Steve, thoughts on that?

01:11:51.120 --> 01:11:56.250
Several trends in the overall historical archival
of disbelief when there's a lot of religions

01:11:56.250 --> 01:11:58.830
have become more humanistic.

01:11:58.830 --> 01:12:03.270
They don't take their literal beliefs as seriously
as they used to.

01:12:03.270 --> 01:12:07.050
If you're a real, believing Christian and
that if you don't accept Jesus then you're

01:12:07.050 --> 01:12:10.870
going to go to hell, then you really ought
to try to convert people at sword point.

01:12:10.870 --> 01:12:12.860
And you really ought to slay heretics.

01:12:12.860 --> 01:12:14.840
You'd be doing ... It's like a great public
health measure.

01:12:14.840 --> 01:12:19.000
You're saving eternity of suffering in hell
for billions of people.

01:12:19.000 --> 01:12:22.750
But most Christians, no matter how seriously
they take their belief, don't try to convert

01:12:22.750 --> 01:12:24.070
people at sword point anymore.

01:12:24.070 --> 01:12:29.420
They don't have inquisitions and they're not
completely consistent and that is a kind of

01:12:29.420 --> 01:12:35.050
benign hypocrisy among many believers that
they fortunately don't act on the totality

01:12:35.050 --> 01:12:38.400
of their religious beliefs and that's been
a very beneficial trend.

01:12:38.400 --> 01:12:45.430
The institutions persist with the all-encompassing
nature of the beliefs, we get deluded.

01:12:45.430 --> 01:12:51.090
Another is that when people switch their religious
affiliations, the overwhelming tendency is

01:12:51.090 --> 01:12:54.920
toward no religion at all, so the world is
becoming less religious.

01:12:54.920 --> 01:12:59.080
There are two reasons why that may seem hard
to believe.

01:12:59.080 --> 01:13:03.190
One of them is that religious people have
more babies and so the number or religious

01:13:03.190 --> 01:13:08.590
people is actually increasing, and projected
to increase even as the number of people who

01:13:08.590 --> 01:13:11.800
switch are switched in the direction of no
religion.

01:13:11.800 --> 01:13:16.630
The other is that religious groups tend to
be more politically organized.

01:13:16.630 --> 01:13:23.010
So, the problem with secularists and humanists,
and so called nones, N-O-N-E, not N-U-N, that

01:13:23.010 --> 01:13:27.640
is people with no religion, is they don't
vote.

01:13:27.640 --> 01:13:28.760
Evangelicals all vote.

01:13:28.760 --> 01:13:30.750
I shouldn't say all.

01:13:30.750 --> 01:13:37.750
Something like 80% of evangelicals vote, 25%
of just the unaffiliated vote.

01:13:37.750 --> 01:13:44.679
And so there's a outsized influence of religion
in politics because of this organization.

01:13:44.679 --> 01:13:51.300
Our perception of the growing influence of
religion is in, not exactly an illusion, but

01:13:51.300 --> 01:13:56.190
it is pushed along by the greater fecundity
and greater political organization of the

01:13:56.190 --> 01:14:01.630
religious, even as the overall direction is
away from religious belief with secularization,

01:14:01.630 --> 01:14:07.070
including the United States, which was a ... for
a long time was an outlier that every other

01:14:07.070 --> 01:14:11.430
western democracy had become less religious
than the United States.

01:14:11.430 --> 01:14:15.730
The United States is now moving in that direction
as well.

01:14:15.730 --> 01:14:21.721
One final question which is sort of the inverse
of the topic that took up some of our time

01:14:21.721 --> 01:14:25.730
in thinking about animals and their reactions
and beliefs.

01:14:25.730 --> 01:14:27.940
What if we flip it the other way?

01:14:27.940 --> 01:14:35.679
So, a hundred years from now, or 500 years
from now, we get visited by an alien civilization

01:14:35.679 --> 01:14:40.710
and we show them what we've learned in math
and physics and they nod their tentacles and

01:14:40.710 --> 01:14:43.500
they ... you know, we're all sort of good.

01:14:43.500 --> 01:14:48.150
But then we show them our religious beliefs.

01:14:48.150 --> 01:14:51.000
Do you think that they'll look at that and
say, "Yeah, yeah, we get it.

01:14:51.000 --> 01:14:55.520
You know, we've got out Jesus too."

01:14:55.520 --> 01:15:01.410
Or will they be completely baffled as to what
this thing called religion is?

01:15:01.410 --> 01:15:03.670
Barbara, thoughts on that?

01:15:03.670 --> 01:15:04.860
Wild speculation.

01:15:04.860 --> 01:15:07.680
I have no idea the answer to that question.

01:15:07.680 --> 01:15:08.680
Fair enough.

01:15:08.680 --> 01:15:13.960
If I had to guess, I would guess baffled.

01:15:13.960 --> 01:15:23.010
I think that if you think about how 500 years
ago, we didn't know much about electromagnetism

01:15:23.010 --> 01:15:24.550
right?

01:15:24.550 --> 01:15:29.810
And now, we can go all kinds of things with
and we can actually entertain ourselves with.

01:15:29.810 --> 01:15:34.630
Well, think of the aliens come and they understand
the function of consciousness in the universe,

01:15:34.630 --> 01:15:35.630
right?

01:15:35.630 --> 01:15:39.260
And they can use consciousness that we use
for all kinds of things.

01:15:39.260 --> 01:15:41.340
It's not any kind of mysterious thing for
them.

01:15:41.340 --> 01:15:44.130
That's I think where it's going.

01:15:44.130 --> 01:15:45.130
Lisa?

01:15:45.130 --> 01:15:49.070
I don't think they'll be baffled and I don't
think that they'll necessarily share ... I

01:15:49.070 --> 01:15:54.170
mean, I'm just ... I'm not even speculating,
I'm imagining.

01:15:54.170 --> 01:16:02.690
I think that they will see it as part of the
evolutionary trajectory of ... Or evolutionary

01:16:02.690 --> 01:16:15.670
development of a species and maybe something
that was a necessary step along the way, but

01:16:15.670 --> 01:16:18.020
became unnecessary at a certain point.

01:16:18.020 --> 01:16:20.710
Final thoughts on that one, Steve?

01:16:20.710 --> 01:16:21.750
I tend to agree there.

01:16:21.750 --> 01:16:27.330
It may be similar to our attitudes towards
the animistic beliefs of people that we've

01:16:27.330 --> 01:16:29.120
come across.

01:16:29.120 --> 01:16:33.040
We can ... or an intelligible but we might
consider them obsolete.

01:16:33.040 --> 01:16:35.910
Do you think that they will have had a similar
evolutionary trajectory?

01:16:35.910 --> 01:16:41.540
I know there ... Is this an intrinsic part
of the way in which a living system would

01:16:41.540 --> 01:16:48.489
evolve that can survive that will necessarily
ascribe agency in the world and tell stories

01:16:48.489 --> 01:16:53.380
about what those agents do and the role that
they play or is this some peculiar thing that

01:16:53.380 --> 01:16:55.239
happened to the human species?

01:16:55.239 --> 01:17:01.600
A great question, a profound one, but I suspect
that ... I guess the question is, does sociality

01:17:01.600 --> 01:17:09.640
depend on reciprocal mentalizing, attributing
complexity to other creatures?

01:17:09.640 --> 01:17:16.110
I suspect it does, but a speculation and if
so, would there be enough evidence early enough

01:17:16.110 --> 01:17:24.980
in the history of an intelligent species that
it would not be tempted by over-attribution

01:17:24.980 --> 01:17:30.270
of cognitive organization to entities that
may not actually have it.

01:17:30.270 --> 01:17:31.270
Very good.

01:17:31.270 --> 01:17:34.300
So, one day we may find the answer to that
question, but until that time, please join

01:17:34.300 --> 01:17:44.060
me in thanking the group here.

